IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- ============================================================================
-- gdpr-core.l4 -- THE NOUNS OF REGULATION (EU) 2016/679 (GDPR), SHARED BY EVERY PART.
-- Written by P1 (Sonnet 5.5) as the keel.  DECLARE only: no rule, no #EVAL, no #ASSERT.
-- Source text: the Publications Office consolidated version of 4.5.2016 (it applies the corrigendum
-- of OJ L 127, 23.5.2018, p. 2).  It is a documentation tool with no legal effect; the Official
-- Journal text is authentic.  Target compiler: main (build 1.6.112).  No date axis: the text
-- encoded is the one text in force from 25 May 2018 (Art 99(2); assumed, not ruled).
--
-- WHAT BELONGS HERE: a fact a witness of ordinary competence could testify to, or an enumeration
-- the text itself makes (the six lawful bases, the ten grounds, the categories Art 9(1) names)
-- that two parts would otherwise invent twice.  Whether a thing is "personal data", "processing",
-- a "controller" or a "processor" is a rule and lives in gdpr-definitions.l4.
--
-- NAMING: a name taken from a provision begins with that provision's label (`Art 6(1)(a) ...`);
-- the role nouns and the two shared result types have plain names that no later part may reuse.
-- Money would be a NUMBER and a date a `Date day month year`; nothing in Article 2, 4 (points 1,
-- 2, 6, 7, 8), 5 to 11 or 24 is a sum of money or a date, so none is declared here.
-- ============================================================================

§ `The roles the text names`

-- A party the Regulation speaks of (Art 4(1), (7)-(10), (17), (21); Art 8 for the child).
-- This is the PARTY type for any regulative rule of any part that wants one shared actor.
DECLARE `Role in the Regulation` IS ONE OF
    `data subject`
    `controller`
    `joint controller`
    `processor`
    `recipient`
    `third party`
    `representative`
    `supervisory authority`
    `child`

-- "natural or legal person, public authority, agency or other body" (Art 4(7), (8), (9), (10)).
-- The last member is a case just outside the list, for example a software tool or an animal.
DECLARE `Kind of person or body` IS ONE OF
    `natural person`
    `legal person`
    `public authority`
    `agency`
    `other body`
    `not a person or body`

-- One answer for every duty that has a gate ("where", "if", "when", "where necessary"); NOTHING
-- means "not covered".  `the duty does not arise` is the text's own no to a failed gate (periods
-- ruling (b) of 2026-11-05); `the duty arises and is not met` is the provision's own question
-- answered no.
DECLARE `Duty outcome` IS ONE OF
    `the duty does not arise`
    `the duty arises and is met`
    `the duty arises and is not met`

§ `Article 4: what the words of the definitions are made of`

-- Art 4(1): "identified, directly or indirectly, in particular by reference to an identifier such as ..."
DECLARE `Art 4(1) means of identification` IS ONE OF
    `Art 4(1) a name`
    `Art 4(1) an identification number`
    `Art 4(1) location data`
    `Art 4(1) an online identifier`
    `Art 4(1) a factor specific to the physical identity`
    `Art 4(1) a factor specific to the physiological identity`
    `Art 4(1) a factor specific to the genetic identity`
    `Art 4(1) a factor specific to the mental identity`
    `Art 4(1) a factor specific to the economic identity`
    `Art 4(1) a factor specific to the cultural identity`
    `Art 4(1) a factor specific to the social identity`
    `Art 4(1) some other means of identification`

-- Art 4(2): the operations "such as ..."; the last member is an operation the list does not name.
DECLARE `Art 4(2) operation on personal data` IS ONE OF
    `Art 4(2) collection`
    `Art 4(2) recording`
    `Art 4(2) organisation`
    `Art 4(2) structuring`
    `Art 4(2) storage`
    `Art 4(2) adaptation`
    `Art 4(2) alteration`
    `Art 4(2) retrieval`
    `Art 4(2) consultation`
    `Art 4(2) use`
    `Art 4(2) disclosure by transmission`
    `Art 4(2) dissemination`
    `Art 4(2) otherwise making available`
    `Art 4(2) alignment`
    `Art 4(2) combination`
    `Art 4(2) restriction`
    `Art 4(2) erasure`
    `Art 4(2) destruction`
    `Art 4(2) some other operation`

-- Art 4(6): "whether centralised, decentralised or dispersed on a functional or geographical basis".
DECLARE `Art 4(6) arrangement of a set of personal data` IS ONE OF
    `Art 4(6) centralised`
    `Art 4(6) decentralised`
    `Art 4(6) dispersed on a functional basis`
    `Art 4(6) dispersed on a geographical basis`
    `Art 4(6) arranged in some other way`

-- Art 4(7): "alone or jointly with others".
DECLARE `Art 4(7) how a controller determines the purposes and means` IS ONE OF
    `Art 4(7) alone`
    `Art 4(7) jointly with others`

§ `Chapter II and Article 24: the enumerations the text makes`

-- Art 5(1)(a) to (f) and Art 5(2): the seven names the text gives in brackets.
DECLARE `Art 5 principle` IS ONE OF
    `Art 5(1)(a) lawfulness, fairness and transparency`
    `Art 5(1)(b) purpose limitation`
    `Art 5(1)(c) data minimisation`
    `Art 5(1)(d) accuracy`
    `Art 5(1)(e) storage limitation`
    `Art 5(1)(f) integrity and confidentiality`
    `Art 5(2) accountability`

-- "archiving purposes in the public interest, scientific or historical research purposes or statistical
-- purposes": the same four purposes in Art 5(1)(b), Art 5(1)(e), Art 9(2)(j) and (for P9) Art 89.
DECLARE `Art 5(1)(b) purpose of archiving, research or statistics` IS ONE OF
    `Art 5(1)(b) archiving purposes in the public interest`
    `Art 5(1)(b) scientific research purposes`
    `Art 5(1)(b) historical research purposes`
    `Art 5(1)(b) statistical purposes`
    `Art 5(1)(b) some other purpose`

-- The six lawful bases of Art 6(1) (the chapeau: "at least one of the following applies").
DECLARE `Art 6(1) lawful basis` IS ONE OF
    `Art 6(1)(a) consent`
    `Art 6(1)(b) contract`
    `Art 6(1)(c) legal obligation`
    `Art 6(1)(d) vital interests`
    `Art 6(1)(e) public interest or official authority`
    `Art 6(1)(f) legitimate interests`

-- The categories of Art 9(1).  The first four are data "revealing" the thing; the biometric member
-- is special only "for the purpose of uniquely identifying a natural person", so biometric data
-- used for another purpose is the member just outside it.  The last member is every other data.
DECLARE `Art 9(1) category of personal data` IS ONE OF
    `Art 9(1) data revealing racial or ethnic origin`
    `Art 9(1) data revealing political opinions`
    `Art 9(1) data revealing religious or philosophical beliefs`
    `Art 9(1) data revealing trade union membership`
    `Art 9(1) genetic data`
    `Art 9(1) biometric data for the purpose of uniquely identifying a natural person`
    `Art 9(1) data concerning health`
    `Art 9(1) data concerning a natural person's sex life or sexual orientation`
    `Art 9(1) biometric data not for the purpose of uniquely identifying a natural person`
    `Art 9(1) other personal data`

-- The ten grounds of Art 9(2), (a) to (j).
DECLARE `Art 9(2) ground` IS ONE OF
    `Art 9(2)(a) explicit consent`
    `Art 9(2)(b) employment and social security and social protection law`
    `Art 9(2)(c) vital interests where the data subject cannot give consent`
    `Art 9(2)(d) a not-for-profit body and its members`
    `Art 9(2)(e) data manifestly made public by the data subject`
    `Art 9(2)(f) legal claims or courts acting in their judicial capacity`
    `Art 9(2)(g) substantial public interest`
    `Art 9(2)(h) health or social care`
    `Art 9(2)(i) public interest in the area of public health`
    `Art 9(2)(j) archiving, research or statistical purposes`
